INT. COIN LAUNDROMAT - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent hum. Rows of orange plastic chairs. Forty dryers, all still. Rain streaks the plate glass.

LINH TRAN, 32, wiry, scrubs under a parka, stands over a washer holding a pewter urn against her chest. Her brother BAO, 28, in a rumpled suit, blocks the aisle.

BAO
Give it to me.

LINH
You said you'd drive it to Da Nang. You haven't left the city in nine years.

At the folding table, their aunt DI HOA, 60s, in a quilted housecoat, folds the same towel again and again.

BAO
Mom wanted it scattered. Not carried around in a laundromat.

LINH
She wanted it by her sister.

DI HOA
(without looking up)
She wanted quiet.

They both turn. Bao lunges for the urn. Linh pivots; the urn swings between them, lid chattering. They both freeze, breath held.

MR. FAIRWEATHER, 70s, the night attendant, cardigan and slippers, shuffles out of the back with a mop and a bucket.

FAIRWEATHER
Folks. Closing in ten.

LINH
We're not done.

FAIRWEATHER
Ten minutes.

Bao and Linh drop their hands at the same moment. The lid slips and lands on the tile with a loud, clear ring. Nobody moves.

Di Hoa stands, steps over, and picks it up. She sets it on the lid of the washer, puts the urn back on top, and clicks it shut.

DI HOA
Both of you. Sit.

They sit. The rain keeps falling.
